    "texts":[
        {
            "texttype":"Online",
            "textpurpose":"Provenance",
            "textentryhtml":"<p>\n\tBy February 15, 1965, Gillett G. Griffin (1928-2016), Princeton, NJ [1]; 2016, bequeathed to the Princeton University Art Museum.\n</p>\n<p>\n\tNotes:\n\t<br />\n\t[1] Included in “Ancient America: Five Centuries of Discovery,” Princeton University Library, February 15 – April 15, 1965, as demonstrated by installation photographs in the curatorial files.\n</p>",
            "remarks":null
        },
        {
            "texttype":"Online",
            "textpurpose":"Gallery Label",
            "textentryhtml":"Ceramic urns, one of the best-known types of small-scale Beni Zaa art, were usually placed in thematic groups within tombs. Some urns represent Beni Zaa deities, while others portray historical individuals, possibly identiﬁed as members of lineages or social groups by their elaborate headdresses or the addition of hieroglyphic names. This particular human representation wears a headdress that includes a feline mouth, with a curling element like a proboscis above, ﬂanked by feather-adorned eyes. Contemporaneous to this vessel from Oaxaca, the fantastic feathered jaquar-butterfly face is also seen at Teotihuacan, an urban center in central Mexico. In both locations, the figure seems to be associated with warfare. Both urban centers created assemblages of hand-modeled and mold-made elements for complex ceramic works.\n",
            "remarks":"AAA3_20-T6-3_CLA_FA.pdf  - Day 1 installation"
        }
    ],
